 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What's going on with Tay-Sachs",
   "text": "Tay-Sachs happens because of changes in a gene called HEXA, which is a tiny piece of instructions inside the body's cells (a paper reports [Eae05983], a paper reports [Ea4cf63d]). HEXA normally helps build an enzyme, which is like a tiny tool that breaks things down inside cells (a paper reports [Ebfd4d0a], a paper reports [Edf59bbd]). When this tool is missing or broken, cells cannot clear away a fatty substance called GM2 ganglioside, so it piles up inside nerve cells (a paper reports [E4417428], a paper reports [Ee89b775]). This kind of problem is called a lysosomal enzyme deficiency, meaning a clean-up center inside the cell called the lysosome is short of a needed tool (a paper reports [E87925e8], a paper reports [Eed205de]). This buildup has also been linked with extra activity of certain brain support cells, called neuroinflammation (a paper reports [Ebda5e0f]). It has also been linked with the loss of nerve cells over time, called neurodegeneration (a paper reports [Eb9e85ed]).",
   "citations": [
