  {
   "key": "similar",
   "title": "Diseases that work the same way",
   "text": "Sandhoff disease shares the same storage problem as Tay-Sachs, where the fatty substance GM2 ganglioside builds up in nerve cells, but it comes from a different gene called HEXB (a paper reports [E279a08b], a paper reports [E0d0eb92], a paper reports [E82f44df], a paper reports [E4dd43d8]). GM2 AB variant shares that same GM2 ganglioside storage problem too, but through a different gene called GM2A (a paper reports [E879b3c8], according to HPO [E2252927]). GM1 (infantile) shares the lysosomal enzyme deficiency mechanism, where a clean-up tool inside the cell is missing, but its gene is called GLB1 instead (a paper reports [E995e5af], according to HPO [E99c0547]).",
