   "key": "what_exists",
   "title": "What exists already",
   "text": "The National Tay-Sachs & Allied Diseases Association is a patient group that works on Tay-Sachs and related diseases (a paper reports [E951b691]). The Cure and Action for Tay-Sachs (CATS) Foundation supports families affected by Tay-Sachs and Sandhoff disease (a paper reports [E99fc35b]). A natural history study called PRONTO, run by Azafaros, follows people with GM1 and GM2 diseases including Tay-Sachs over time to learn more (a paper reports [Ed1705e4], a paper reports [E48e5738]). There is a patient registry called the Tay-Sachs and Sandhoff Disease Registry that collects health information to help research (a paper reports [Eb51957b], a paper reports [E624aa7e]). A clinical trial testing a medicine called nizubaglustat is recruiting people with Tay-Sachs and related GM1 or GM2 forms of disease, according to ClinicalTrials.gov (according to ClinicalTrials.gov [Ee1176a9], according to ClinicalTrials.gov [E05ee7b4]). A research grant called 'Mouse Models of Novel Sphingolipid Biology and Disease Mechanisms,' led by researcher Richard Proia, is listed as studying Tay-Sachs disease, according to NIH RePORTER (according to NIH RePORTER [Eb1e63f1], according to NIH RePORTER [E5894b74]). Researcher Guangping Gao authored a paper that mentions Tay-Sachs disease (we matched by name [E1465917], according to PubMed [E90252db]).",
